direction: Language & Compiler (front / layout)
scope: |
  BUGS v2-front-try-in-def-body-shapes-break — found 2026-07-27 while fixing try/catch delivery.
  TWO shapes of `try` as a DEF BODY break on the v2 front (both fronts; v1 reference is correct):
   (a) wildcard `def w(x) = try { .. } catch { case _ => "W" }` -> the WHOLE program is dropped:
       no output, exit 0 on default/F/bytecode/interpret; legacy front says `unbound global: try`.
       The same wildcard works when the try is an EXPRESSION ARGUMENT, and the same def with
       `case e: Throwable` works — so the trigger is bare `case _` in a def-body try.
   (b) braceless multiline try as a def body does not terminate: a following top-level statement is
       silently dropped, a following def whose body starts with `try` becomes `unbound global: try`.
  WORST PROPERTY: (a) is SILENT at exit 0 on the default lane, and F emits a VALID-but-wrong program
  so the F4a fallback never fires — no existing gate can see it.
  Lead: `unbound global: try` on legacy means `try` was parsed as an IDENTIFIER, i.e. the try-parse
  arm was not entered — start at ssc1-front.ssc0 try parsing (~:1077-1101) + the layout/semicolon
  inference that the curried-def and layer-1 bugs also hit.
  Repro (3 lines) and the full lane table are in the BUGS entry.
